\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Exterior transport preserves the pairing}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The \vocabk{exterior rotational action}{fdip-00YI} is a
representation of #{H} by automorphisms preserving #{B}. Every
scalar unit belonging to #{H}, in particular #{-1} when present,
acts trivially.}

\proof{
\p{The identity and composition laws hold on every pure wedge
because #{\rho(RS)=\rho(R)\rho(S)} by \ref{fdip-00OB}.
Pure wedges span each exterior power, so
#{A(RS)=A(R)A(S)}, #{A(1)=\bbI_E} and
#{A(R)^{-1}=A(R^{-1})}. In the determinant defining #{B}, replacing
#{v_i,w_j} by #{\rho(R)v_i,\rho(R)w_j} leaves every entry
unchanged. The action preserves degree, so it also preserves the
orthogonality between different degrees. Finally
#{\rho(\lambda)=\bbI_V} for each scalar unit #{\lambda}, again
by \ref{fdip-00OB}. Its exterior action is therefore the identity.}
}

\p{The exterior composition step is the component
\leanref{ExteriorAlgebra.map_comp_map}; the induced exterior-power
representation is the component
\leanref/tauceti{Representation.exteriorPower_apply}.
Neither component supplies the perfect pairing or the affine links.}

\p{The form #{b} was declared separately: the Clifford relations
of \ref{fdip-00O5} do not select a perfect integral symmetric form
from an arbitrary #{q}. The action here is the exterior action
of the vector representation. It makes no choice of a spinor module.}
